{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is a distributed web crawler?

A distributed web crawler is a fleet of programs that discovers web addresses, decides which address is safe and useful to visit next, downloads a response, and uses any accepted links to continue the loop. Search engines use crawlers to build the collection of documents that an index can later search.

In plain language, a crawler is a careful map maker. Its central invariant is: **a URL may be fetched only after it is normalized, deduplicated, allowed by the host's policy, and eligible under that host's crawl budget.** More workers can increase global throughput, but they must never turn one busy or failing website into a victim of the crawl.

This case study designs a crawl system for one billion pages each month. You will move from the crawl contract and capacity math to the frontier state machine, polite failure paths, operations, and the trade-offs behind a responsible crawler.
{% /section-card %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify the crawl contract before choosing queues" %}

## Define what the crawler is allowed to do

A crawler does not own the sites it visits. The first design decision is therefore a behavioral contract, not a worker count: discover useful public documents while placing an explicit, bounded load on each remote host.

### Functional scope

- Start from supplied seed URLs and discover links from accepted HTML documents.
- Fetch HTML and selected documents such as PDFs; treat rendering-heavy pages as an explicit later capability.
- Normalize URLs, record redirects, suppress repeat work, and schedule refreshes.
- Read and cache `robots.txt`; honor applicable allow/disallow rules and configured crawl delays.

### Non-functional guardrails

- **Scale:** crawl one billion pages per month, with headroom for daily spikes.
- **Politeness:** a host's rules and health always override a URL's global priority.
- **Safety:** bound redirects, response size, URL length, traversal depth, parser time, and retries.
- **Recoverability:** isolate a bad host or failed worker so unrelated hosts continue making progress.

{% callout variant="info" %}
`robots.txt` is a crawl-policy signal, not an authorization system. A production crawler still needs a recognizable User-Agent, a contact channel, retention policy, and legal review appropriate to its data use.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="scale" title="2. Turn the monthly target into an operating envelope" %}

## Estimate the global rate, then remember the host limit

One billion pages over a 30-day month is about **386 pages per second** on average. Designing for a two-times burst gives a planning rate near 800 pages per second. That rate is small enough for a worker fleet, but it says almost nothing about whether any individual host may be contacted now.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="386/s" label="Average fetches" detail="1B pages divided across 30 days." tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="800/s" label="Planning peak" detail="Two-times headroom for uneven discovery and recovery." tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric value="400 TB" label="Compressed bodies" detail="Assume 400 KB average retained response before replicas." tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="160 MB/s" label="Peak ingress" detail="800 fetches/s times 200 KB transferred on average." tone="green" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

The numbers are estimates, not service-level objectives. A 400 KB retained body and 200 KB transferred response can both be true after compression and filtering. Store URL state separately from bodies: URL metadata, hashes, timestamps, and host eligibility are queried far more often than old document bytes.

### Capacity decisions that follow from the estimate

- Partition ownership by a stable host or registrable-domain hash, so one scheduler owns its politeness state.
- Keep enough ready hosts to fill workers when many high-priority hosts are waiting for their next allowed time.
- Autoscale on eligible frontier depth and processing lag, not raw discovered-URL count; blocked URLs are not immediate work.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Separate scheduling, fetching, and processing" %}

## Keep the fetch path short and the discovery loop durable

The crawler has two loops. The synchronous loop determines whether a single URL may be fetched now. The asynchronous loop turns an accepted response into storage records and new candidate URLs. Separating them prevents slow parsing or object storage from holding a host's crawl slot.

{% system-flow title="One responsible crawl cycle" caption="The frontier grants work only after the host is eligible. Parsing produces candidates asynchronously, which return through normalization before they can be scheduled." %}
{% system-node title="Normalize and deduplicate" eyebrow="Admission" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
Canonicalize safe URL parts, reject unsupported schemes, and check seen-URL state.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Frontier and host queue" eyebrow="Schedule" icon="timer" tone="violet" %}
Rank eligible work while maintaining a separate next-allowed time for every host.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Fetch with limits" eyebrow="Acquire" icon="network" tone="cyan" %}
Apply robots policy, timeouts, response caps, redirect bounds, and bounded retries.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Parse, fingerprint, persist" eyebrow="Expand" icon="database" tone="green" %}
Store accepted content, detect duplicate bodies, and return extracted links to admission.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Component responsibilities

| Component | Owns | Must not do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Admission service | URL normalization, scheme policy, exact seen state | Grant a fetch before host eligibility is checked |
| URL frontier | Priority, freshness, host ownership, next-allowed time | Parse large document bodies |
| Fetch worker | HTTP, redirects, response limits, result classification | Independently override a host delay |
| Processor | Parsing, content hashes, link extraction, storage handoff | Reinsert raw links without admission |

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="frontier" title="4. Trace the frontier state instead of treating it as one queue" %}

## A URL has state before it has a worker

The frontier is a policy engine. It combines global value, freshness, duplicate state, and a host-specific timer. Select each scenario below, then select a component to inspect its responsibility. The active path shows why a newly discovered URL, a duplicate, and a robots-blocked URL do not consume the same resources.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/case-studies/web-crawler/data/frontier-state-topology.json" /%}

### The frontier's useful state record

- **URL key:** normalized URL, redirect aliases, discovery source, and exact seen status.
- **Host key:** registrable domain or host, policy cache expiry, next allowed fetch time, and failure history.
- **Scheduling key:** priority score, last successful fetch, predicted change rate, and a lease that prevents duplicate worker assignment.

Use a fast probabilistic filter to reject likely repeats before an exact backing lookup. A Bloom-filter false positive can delay discovery, so protect important seed and recrawl candidates with an exact store or a second-chance policy.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="politeness" title="5. Make politeness and failure paths visible" %}

## A high-priority URL is still allowed to wait

Politeness is enforced at the host scheduler, before a worker opens a connection. That decision keeps a fresh news page from bypassing a crawl delay and makes retries a controlled state transition rather than a request storm.

Select a healthy path, a crawl-delay wait, a robots denial, or a timeout. The scenario changes the active route and outcome; selecting nodes explains where the decision is owned.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/case-studies/web-crawler/data/politeness-failure-topology.json" /%}

### Failure policy

- **Retry only classified transient failures:** network resets, 429s, and selected 5xx responses qualify; policy denials and most 4xx responses do not.
- **Back off per host:** increase the host's next-allowed time with jitter, and open a circuit after repeated failures.
- **Bound the attempt:** cap DNS, connect, first-byte, total download, redirect count, and body size independently.
- **Preserve evidence:** record the status, policy version, response timing, and retry reason before sending exhausted work to a review or replay queue.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Never recover a fleet-wide throughput dip by removing host delays. The crawler can borrow capacity only from other eligible hosts, not from a remote server's patience.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="defenses" title="6. Defend capacity against duplicates, traps, and bad content" %}

## Stop unbounded work before it becomes a scale problem

The web contains aliases, calendars, faceted navigation, session IDs, infinite paths, malformed documents, and intentionally hostile responses. Each defense has a different job; no single URL hash is enough.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="URL duplicates" eyebrow="Before fetch" icon="repeat" tone="blue" %}
Normalize fragments and safe parameters, track redirects, and perform exact seen checks after a probabilistic prefilter.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Content duplicates" eyebrow="After fetch" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Use a cryptographic body hash for exact copies and a near-duplicate fingerprint for template-heavy mirrors.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Crawler traps" eyebrow="Before expansion" icon="warning" tone="amber" %}
Cap depth, URL length, parameter growth, unique paths per host, and repeated structural patterns before adding links.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Defensive defaults

1. Reject unsupported schemes and URLs over the configured size limit before they enter durable state.
2. Enforce per-response byte and parser CPU limits, then classify the failure without retaining unsafe payloads by default.
3. Detect repeated path templates and high-cardinality parameter patterns per host; quarantine the pattern rather than banning the entire site immediately.
4. Keep a dead-letter stream for policy exceptions, persistent parser failures, and manually reviewed traps.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="operations" title="7. Operate the crawler as a set of host-level budgets" %}

## Monitor whether the crawler is useful and safe

Global fetches per second can look healthy while the crawler ignores fresh sites, hammers a small host set, or sends most bytes to duplicates. Operators need metrics that reveal the frontier's decisions and the remote impact of those decisions.

### Metrics that should page, freeze, or trend

- **Page:** policy-cache corruption, a large increase in requests after robots denials, scheduler lease duplication, or an emergency host complaint.
- **Freeze new work for a host:** sustained 429/5xx rates, circuit breaker opening, robots parse ambiguity, or rapidly growing trap signatures.
- **Trend:** eligible frontier age by priority, host-delay compliance, duplicate suppression rate, bytes retained per accepted document, retry exhaustion, and processor lag.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Observe" label="Signals" %}
Measure host eligibility, fetch outcome, byte cost, extracted-link yield, and freshness lag with host and policy dimensions.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Classify" label="Decision" %}
Separate policy blocks, permanent failures, transient failures, duplicate work, traps, and processor defects.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Contain" label="Action" %}
Delay, circuit-break, quarantine, or reduce priority for the smallest affected host or URL pattern.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Recover" label="Review" %}
Replay bounded dead-letter work after the policy, parser, or remote-host condition has changed.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="8. Choose explicit trade-offs and the next maturity step" %}

## Optimize for useful coverage, not raw request volume

There is no universally correct crawl order. The right choice depends on whether the product values breaking news, broad coverage, archival retention, or low operational risk. Record the trade-off so operators do not quietly optimize the wrong number.

| Decision | Conservative default | Cost | Escalate when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| URL deduplication | Probabilistic filter plus exact backing store | More lookup latency and state | High-value URLs are delayed by false positives |
| Host ownership | Stable domain-hash partition | Rebalancing can move policy state | A few hosts create hot partitions |
| Freshness | Recrawl from observed change history | New pages may wait longer | Product needs near-real-time coverage |
| Rendering | Fetch raw responses first | Misses client-rendered links | Evidence shows important coverage gaps |

### Risks and extensions

- Add a renderer queue only after it has a distinct budget, sandbox, response limit, and per-host concurrency policy; browser rendering must not silently borrow ordinary fetch capacity.
- Move from static scores to learned crawl priority only when logged outcomes can show that freshness improves without reducing host-delay compliance.
- Support content deletion, retention, and access-request workflows before expanding long-term body storage.

The durable design lesson is simple: the frontier decides what the fleet is permitted to do next. Its host-level state is as important as its global priority score.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% /accordion %}

